🚀 Mastering Redis: Understanding Eviction Policies 🧠

Working with Redis? One of the most crucial aspects of managing memory effectively is choosing the right eviction policy — especially when your instance reaches its maxmemory limit.

Here’s a quick breakdown of what’s available:

🔹 noeviction – Stops writes when memory is full. Safe, but not ideal for caching.
🔹 allkeys-lru – Evicts least recently used keys from all keys. Great for cache-heavy systems.
🔹 volatile-lru – Same as above, but only affects keys with an expiration (TTL).
🔹 allkeys-random / volatile-random – Evicts random keys (from all or just expirable ones).
🔹 volatile-ttl – Evicts keys about to expire first.
🔹 allkeys-lfu / volatile-lfu – Target least frequently used keys. Excellent for persistent hot/cold data use.

🔧 Pro tip: Combine a smart eviction policy with memory tuning (maxmemory) to ensure high performance and stability.

Whether you're optimizing a real-time leaderboard, caching microservices responses, or handling session state — your choice here matters.

